IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g6-interpretation`
IMPORT `g6-art15-16-deregistration`

-- g6-art17-after-deregistration.l4: Article 17 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014, Effects of deregistration.
-- Inputs from other groups (PLAN section (c)): IP7 `the registered charity` (G3; the fields registered charitable purposes and registered public benefit statement are the preserved ones),
-- IP12 `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` (G1; applied for Article 17(9)(a) as if the entity had not been deregistered), IP31 the type `Order of the court over a charity` (G5, domain.l4).
-- Outputs: the date from which a termination takes effect, from which G3 sets the field `date deregistered` of `the registered charity` (IP7).
-- A discretion is an input: "satisfied", "determines", "considers expedient", "appears to the court" and "necessary or desirable" are determinations the caller supplies.
-- Dates are DATEs. Periods in years are computed with the helper of g6-interpretation (`add years` clamps 29 February; the readings of F6.14 and F6.18 choose among three conventions).

§ `Part 4 -- Registration of charities`

§§ `Article 17 -- Effects of deregistration`

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The nouns of this Article (group-local; see FRAGMENT-G6.md section (4))
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- What the Commissioner does after deregistering, value-actor style.
DECLARE `Act under Article 17` IS ONE OF
    `discard the information registered in respect of the entity` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `assign the registered number of the entity to another registered charity` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `make an amending order` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `make an order ancillary to an amending order` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `exercise a power of Article 14 over the deregistered entity` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
                                                                , order IS AN `Order of the court over a charity`
    `apply to the court under paragraph (9)` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `take over the application of a governor` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `withdraw the application` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`
    `proceed with the application` HAS by IS A `Legal actor`

-- The entity after its deregistration is no longer a registered charity, and domain.l4's `Legal actor` has no role for it; this stand-in is the party of the duties and powers of paragraphs (6) to (8)
-- (nouns request in FRAGMENT-G6.md section (4)).
DECLARE `Party after deregistration` IS ONE OF
    `the entity after its deregistration`

DECLARE `Act of the deregistered entity` IS ONE OF
    `apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement` HAS by IS A `Party after deregistration`
    `apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or the preserved public benefit statement` HAS by IS A `Party after deregistration`

-- How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity: the facts Article 17 reads. The deregistration is "by written notice" under Article 16 (or by a decision on an application under Article 15).
DECLARE `Deregistration of the entity` HAS
    route IS A `Route by which the Commissioner deregistered the entity`
        @desc How the Commissioner deregistered the entity (on application under Article 15, or under Article 16(1) or (2))
    `date of the deregistration` IS A DATE
        @desc The date of the written notice by which the Commissioner deregistered the entity, or of the decision on an application under Article 15
    `date of the preliminary notice` IS A MAYBE DATE
        @desc The date of the preliminary notice of Article 16(1) giving reasons and inviting objections; NOTHING where there was none (an application, or Article 16(2))
    `the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds have existed since` IS A MAYBE DATE
        @desc The past date since which the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed (Article 17(2)(b)); NOTHING if not so satisfied
    `past date specified in the deregistration notice` IS A MAYBE DATE
        @desc The past date the notice by which the Commissioner deregistered specifies (Article 17(2)(c)); NOTHING if it specifies none
    `past date specified in the preliminary notice` IS A MAYBE DATE
        @desc The past date the preliminary notice specifies (read only under the reading that "the notice" is the preliminary notice); NOTHING if it specifies none
    `the Commissioner determines that the deregistration takes effect retrospectively` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the Commissioner has determined that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively (Article 17(2)(d))

-- A piece of the charity's property for paragraph (5): when it was acquired and whether it remains.
DECLARE `Property of the charity` HAS
    description IS A STRING
        @desc What the property is
    `acquired on` IS A DATE
        @desc The day the charity acquired it
    remains IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the property still remains the charity's

-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The two notices, and the dates they give
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- F6.3: "the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)" in paragraphs (2)(c) and (4). Article 16(1) has two notices: the preliminary notice (reasons, objections) and the written notice by which the Commissioner deregisters;
-- Article 16(2) has the one. Reading taken: the notice by which the Commissioner deregisters (the notice under paragraph (1) "by written notice ... deregister", and the only notice under paragraph (2)).
@ref Article 17(2)(c) and (4) (F6.3) (src:449) (src:452)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DATE
`the date of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `which notice Articles 17(2)(c) and 17(4) mean`
    WHEN `the notice by which the Commissioner deregisters` THEN dereg's `date of the deregistration`
    WHEN `the preliminary notice that gives reasons and invites objections`
        THEN fromMaybe (dereg's `date of the deregistration`) (dereg's `date of the preliminary notice`)

@ref Article 17(2)(c) and (4) (F6.3) (src:449) (src:452)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the past date specified in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `which notice Articles 17(2)(c) and 17(4) mean`
    WHEN `the notice by which the Commissioner deregisters` THEN dereg's `past date specified in the deregistration notice`
    WHEN `the preliminary notice that gives reasons and invites objections`
        THEN CONSIDER dereg's `date of the preliminary notice`
             WHEN NOTHING THEN dereg's `past date specified in the deregistration notice`
             WHEN JUST given THEN dereg's `past date specified in the preliminary notice`

§§§ `Article 17(2) and (3) -- retrospective effect`

-- src:447 | (a) deregisters a charity under Article 16;
@ref Article 17(2)(a) (src:447)
GIVEN dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16` dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER dereg's route
    WHEN `on the entity's own application under Article 15(3)(a)` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- (b) is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date. A "past date" is a date before the date of the notice.
-- src:448 | (b) is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date;
@ref Article 17(2)(b) (src:448)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds have existed since a past date` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER dereg's `the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds have existed since`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST since THEN since LESS THAN `the date of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg

-- (c) specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2): the date specified is the date the Commissioner is satisfied of.
-- src:449 | (c) specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2); and
@ref Article 17(2)(c) (src:449)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER `the past date specified in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST specified THEN
        CONSIDER dereg's `the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds have existed since`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST since THEN specified EQUALS since

-- src:450 | (d) determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively.
@ref Article 17(2)(d) (src:450)
GIVEN dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively` dereg MEANS
    dereg's `the Commissioner determines that the deregistration takes effect retrospectively`

-- src:446 | (2) Paragraph (3) applies if the Commissioner –
-- src:447 | (a) deregisters a charity under Article 16;
-- src:448 | (b) is satisfied that the grounds for deregistration have existed since a past date;
-- src:449 | (c) specifies that date in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2); and
-- src:450 | (d) determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively.
@ref Article 17(2) (src:446-450)
@export Whether Article 17(3) applies to a deregistration: the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16, is satisfied the grounds have existed since a past date, specifies that date in the notice, and determines that the deregistration takes effect retrospectively
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3 (which notice is "the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)")
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(2) the deregistration takes effect retrospectively` interp dereg MEANS
        "(2) Paragraph (3) applies if the Commissioner –"
    ... "(a)" ... `Art 17(2)(a) the Commissioner deregisters a charity under Article 16` dereg
    AND "(b)" ... `Art 17(2)(b) the Commissioner is satisfied that the grounds have existed since a past date` interp dereg
    AND "(c)" ... `Art 17(2)(c) the Commissioner specifies that date in the notice` interp dereg
    AND "(d)" ... `Art 17(2)(d) the Commissioner determines that the deregistration should take effect retrospectively` dereg

-- (3) The termination takes effect from the past date specified, for all purposes, including taxation, subject to (4). NOTHING means paragraph (3) does not apply: no past date is fixed by it.
-- src:451 | (3) The termination takes effect from the past date specified under paragraph (2)(c) for all purposes, including the purposes of any enactment relating to taxation, but subject to paragraph (4).
@ref Article 17(3) (src:451)
@export The past date from which a termination takes effect under Article 17(3), if paragraph (2) is satisfied (for all purposes, including the purposes of any enactment relating to taxation, subject to paragraph (4)); NOTHING if paragraph (3) does not apply
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`Art 17(3) the date from which the termination takes effect` interp dereg MEANS
    IF   `Art 17(2) the deregistration takes effect retrospectively` interp dereg
    THEN `the past date specified in the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The date on which the entity is deregistered, for the field `date deregistered` of `the registered charity` (IP7): the past date where paragraph (3) applies; otherwise the date of the deregistration itself
-- (the date of the notice by which the Commissioner deregistered, or of the decision on an application). The Law does not say more about when a deregistration without retrospective effect takes effect.
-- src:451 | (3) The termination takes effect from the past date specified under paragraph (2)(c) for all purposes, including the purposes of any enactment relating to taxation, but subject to paragraph (4).
@ref Article 17(3) (src:451)
@export The date on which the entity is treated as deregistered: the past date of Article 17(3) if the deregistration is retrospective, otherwise the date of the deregistration; for the field date deregistered of the registered charity
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 17(3) the date deregistered` interp dereg MEANS
    fromMaybe (dereg's `date of the deregistration`) (`Art 17(3) the date from which the termination takes effect` interp dereg)

§§§ `Article 17(4) -- an act before the notice is not made an offence by the retrospective effect`

-- (4) An act that occurred before the date of the giving of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2) is not rendered an offence merely by virtue of the operation of paragraph (3).
-- The date is that of the notice F6.3 designates; the act must be BEFORE it, so an act on the day of the notice is not protected. Where paragraph (3) does not operate there is nothing to be saved from.
-- src:452 | (4) An act, that occurred before the date of the giving of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2) is not rendered an offence merely by virtue of the operation of paragraph (3).
@ref Article 17(4) (src:452)
@export Whether an act is protected by Article 17(4): it occurred before the date of the giving of the notice, so it is not rendered an offence merely by the retrospective effect of paragraph (3); trivially so where the deregistration is not retrospective
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3 (which notice's date is meant)
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
      `the date of the act` IS A DATE
        @desc The day the act occurred
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(4) the act is not rendered an offence by the retrospective effect` interp dereg `the date of the act` MEANS
        NOT `Art 17(2) the deregistration takes effect retrospectively` interp dereg
    OR  `the date of the act` LESS THAN `the date of the notice under Article 16(1) or (2)` interp dereg

§§§ `Article 17(1) -- what the Commissioner does after deregistering`

-- F6.5: from when the ten years run. "After deregistering an entity, the Commissioner must retain the information registered in respect of that entity for 10 years": the ten years follow the deregistering.
-- Reading taken: from the date of the deregistration notice (the Commissioner "deregisters" by the notice); the other starts them at the date from which the termination takes effect, which may be earlier.
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (F6.5) (src:444)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DATE
`F6.5 reading A: the ten years run from the date of the deregistration notice` interp dereg MEANS
    dereg's `date of the deregistration`

@ref Article 17(1)(a) (F6.5) (src:444)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DATE
`F6.5 reading B: the ten years run from the date from which the termination takes effect` interp dereg MEANS
    `Art 17(3) the date deregistered` interp dereg

@ref Article 17(1)(a) (F6.5) (src:444)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day on which the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `when the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start`
    WHEN `the ten years run from the date of the deregistration notice`
        THEN `F6.5 reading A: the ten years run from the date of the deregistration notice` interp dereg
    WHEN `the ten years run from the date from which the termination takes effect`
        THEN `F6.5 reading B: the ten years run from the date from which the termination takes effect` interp dereg

-- F6.18: how the ten years are counted from their start (the last day is the anniversary, or the day before it; 29 February falls back or rolls).
-- src:444 | (a) must retain the information registered in respect of that entity for 10 years; and
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (src:444)
@export The last day on which the Commissioner must retain the information registered in respect of a deregistered entity: ten years from the day they start, counted on the reading given
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3 (the effective date), F6.5 (when the ten years start) and F6.18 (how they are counted)
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 17(1)(a) the last day on which the Commissioner must retain the information` interp dereg MEANS
    `the last day of a period of years` (interp's `how the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) are counted`) (`the day on which the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start` interp dereg) 10

-- src:444 | (a) must retain the information registered in respect of that entity for 10 years; and
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (src:444)
@export The first day on which the Commissioner may discard the information registered in respect of a deregistered entity: the day after the last day on which it must be retained
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3, F6.5 and F6.18
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A DATE
`Art 17(1)(a) the Commissioner may discard the information on` interp dereg MEANS
    `the day after` (`Art 17(1)(a) the last day on which the Commissioner must retain the information` interp dereg)

-- "Must retain ... for 10 years" is a continuing duty: its breach is discarding the information while the ten years run. It is therefore a prohibition on discarding, for the period, whose length in days is the distance
-- from the start to the last day. A trace starts at the day the ten years start (AT (Day ...)); the last day is inside the period, as a deadline is.
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (src:444)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the number of days from the start of the ten years to their last day` interp dereg MEANS
    Day (`Art 17(1)(a) the last day on which the Commissioner must retain the information` interp dereg) MINUS Day (`the day on which the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start` interp dereg)

-- src:443 | (1) After deregistering an entity, the Commissioner –
-- src:444 | (a) must retain the information registered in respect of that entity for 10 years; and
@ref Article 17(1)(a) (src:443-444)
@export Whether and how the Commissioner must retain the information registered in respect of a deregistered entity for 10 years after deregistering it (the duty is a prohibition on discarding it while the ten years run; start a trace at the day the ten years start)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.3, F6.5 (when the ten years start) and F6.18 (how they are counted)
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(1)(a) the Commissioner must retain the information for 10 years` interp dereg MEANS
    PARTY  `the Commissioner`
    SHANT  `discard the information registered in respect of the entity` `the Commissioner`
    WITHIN `the number of days from the start of the ten years to their last day` interp dereg
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "Article 17(1)(a): the information registered in respect of the deregistered entity was discarded within the ten years for which it must be retained"

-- F6.6: does the bar on assigning the registered number end? Paragraph (1)(b) is joined to (a) by "and" and states no period. Reading taken: it never ends (the number identifies a charity that was registered,
-- and a number reused would make the retained history ambiguous). The other: it lasts for the ten years of (a).
@ref Article 17(1)(b) (F6.6) (src:445)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`F6.6 reading A: the bar on assigning the registered number has no end` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commissioner`
    SHANT `assign the registered number of the entity to another registered charity` `the Commissioner`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST  BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "Article 17(1)(b): the registered number of the deregistered entity was assigned to another registered charity"

@ref Article 17(1)(b) (F6.6) (src:445)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`F6.6 reading B: the bar on assigning the registered number lasts for the ten years of Article 17(1)(a)` interp dereg MEANS
    PARTY  `the Commissioner`
    SHANT  `assign the registered number of the entity to another registered charity` `the Commissioner`
    WITHIN `the number of days from the start of the ten years to their last day` interp dereg
    HENCE  FULFILLED
    LEST   BREACH BY `the Commissioner` BECAUSE "Article 17(1)(b): the registered number of the deregistered entity was assigned to another registered charity within the ten years"

-- The bar as a fact about a day: may the registered number be assigned to another registered charity on this day? (the other face of the prohibition below, for a caller that asks about a day rather than runs a trace)
-- src:445 | (b) must not assign the registered number of that entity to any other registered charity.
@ref Article 17(1)(b) (src:445)
@export Whether the registered number of a deregistered entity is barred from being assigned to another registered charity on a given day: always on the reading taken, and for the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) only on the other
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.6 (how long the bar lasts), and F6.3, F6.5, F6.18 for the ten years
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
        @desc The day on which an assignment of the number is asked about
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(1)(b) the registered number may not be assigned on the day` interp dereg `the day in question` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how long the bar on assigning the registered number lasts`
    WHEN `the bar on assigning the registered number has no end` THEN `the day in question` AT LEAST `the day on which the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start` interp dereg
    WHEN `the bar on assigning the registered number lasts for the ten years of Article 17(1)(a)`
        THEN     `the day in question` AT LEAST `the day on which the ten years of Article 17(1)(a) start` interp dereg
             AND `the day in question` AT MOST `Art 17(1)(a) the last day on which the Commissioner must retain the information` interp dereg

-- src:445 | (b) must not assign the registered number of that entity to any other registered charity.
@ref Article 17(1)(b) (src:445)
@export Whether and how the Commissioner must not assign the registered number of a deregistered entity to any other registered charity; the bar has no end on the reading taken, and lasts ten years on the other (start a trace at the day the ten years start)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.6 (how long the bar lasts), and F6.3, F6.5, F6.18 for the ten years
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(1)(b) the Commissioner must not assign the registered number` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how long the bar on assigning the registered number lasts`
    WHEN `the bar on assigning the registered number has no end`
        THEN `F6.6 reading A: the bar on assigning the registered number has no end`
    WHEN `the bar on assigning the registered number lasts for the ten years of Article 17(1)(a)`
        THEN `F6.6 reading B: the bar on assigning the registered number lasts for the ten years of Article 17(1)(a)` interp dereg

§§§ `Article 17(5) -- the remaining property`

-- F6.4: "any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered". When the deregistration is retrospective the charity is deregistered, "for all purposes" (paragraph (3)), from the past date.
-- Reading taken: "deregistered" means the date from which the termination takes effect, so property acquired on or after that date is not remaining property. The other reading is the date of the notice.
-- "Before" is strict: property acquired on the day itself is not acquired before it.
@ref Article 17(3) and (5) (F6.4) (src:451) (src:453)
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
GIVETH A DATE
`the day before which property must have been acquired to be remaining property` interp dereg MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `the date before which property must have been acquired to be remaining property`
    WHEN `acquired before the date from which the termination takes effect` THEN `Art 17(3) the date deregistered` interp dereg
    WHEN `acquired before the date of the deregistration notice` THEN dereg's `date of the deregistration`

-- src:453 | (5) Paragraphs (6) to (11) apply, after a charity is deregistered, if and so long as there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the “remaining property”).
@ref Article 17(5) (src:453)
@export Whether a piece of the charity's property is "remaining property" under Article 17(5): it was acquired before the charity was deregistered, and it still remains
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.4 (when "deregistered" is), and F6.3
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
      property IS A `Property of the charity`
        @desc The piece of property
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(5) the property is remaining property` interp dereg property MEANS
        property's `acquired on` LESS THAN `the day before which property must have been acquired to be remaining property` interp dereg
    AND property's remains

-- src:453 | (5) Paragraphs (6) to (11) apply, after a charity is deregistered, if and so long as there remains any property of the charity that was acquired before it was deregistered (the “remaining property”).
@ref Article 17(5) (src:453)
@export Whether paragraphs (6) to (11) of Article 17 apply to a deregistered charity: some property acquired before it was deregistered still remains
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 15 to 20`
        @desc The readings of the open points of Articles 15 to 20; here F6.4, and F6.3
      dereg IS A `Deregistration of the entity`
        @desc How and when the Commissioner deregistered the entity
      properties IS A LIST OF `Property of the charity`
        @desc The charity's property
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(5) paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` interp dereg properties MEANS
    any (GIVEN property YIELD `Art 17(5) the property is remaining property` interp dereg property) properties

§§§ `Article 17(6) and (7) -- the preserved purposes, and no power to amend them`

-- The purposes that were the registered charitable purposes "immediately before deregistration" and the public benefit statement then in effect: the fields of the register entry (IP7).
-- src:454 | (6) The entity must continue to apply the remaining property for the purposes that were its registered charitable purposes immediately before deregistration (the “preserved charitable purposes”), and in accordance with the registered public benefit statement that was in effect immediately before deregistration (the “preserved public benefit statement”).
@ref Article 17(6) (src:454)
@export The preserved charitable purposes of a deregistered charity: the purposes that were its registered charitable purposes immediately before deregistration
GIVEN `the registered charity` IS A `Registered charity`
        @desc What the register holds about the entity (IP7)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`Art 17(6) the preserved charitable purposes` `the registered charity` MEANS
    `the registered charity`'s `registered charitable purposes`

-- src:454 | (6) The entity must continue to apply the remaining property for the purposes that were its registered charitable purposes immediately before deregistration (the “preserved charitable purposes”), and in accordance with the registered public benefit statement that was in effect immediately before deregistration (the “preserved public benefit statement”).
@ref Article 17(6) (src:454)
@export The preserved public benefit statement of a deregistered charity: the registered public benefit statement in effect immediately before deregistration
GIVEN `the registered charity` IS A `Registered charity`
        @desc What the register holds about the entity (IP7)
GIVETH A STRING
`Art 17(6) the preserved public benefit statement` `the registered charity` MEANS
    `the registered charity`'s `registered public benefit statement`

-- (6) The entity must continue to apply the remaining property for the preserved purposes and in accordance with the preserved statement. A standing duty: no period, and it lasts "if and so long as" the property remains (paragraph (5)).
-- src:454 | (6) The entity must continue to apply the remaining property for the purposes that were its registered charitable purposes immediately before deregistration (the “preserved charitable purposes”), and in accordance with the registered public benefit statement that was in effect immediately before deregistration (the “preserved public benefit statement”).
@ref Article 17(6) (src:454)
@export Whether and how a deregistered entity must continue to apply its remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement; nothing is owed once no property remains
GIVEN `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether some property acquired before the deregistration still remains (Article 17(5))
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party after deregistration` `Act of the deregistered entity`
`Art 17(6) the entity must apply the remaining property for the preserved purposes` `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` MEANS
    IF   `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply`
    THEN PARTY `the entity after its deregistration`
         MUST  `apply the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement` `the entity after its deregistration`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- (7) Despite any provision of the entity's constitution, or of its constitutional Law, the entity has no power to amend the preserved charitable purposes or the preserved public benefit statement.
-- While paragraphs (6) to (11) apply the answer is FALSE whatever the constitution says; once they do not, the Article says nothing and the constitution decides (an input).
-- src:455 | (7) Despite any provision of the entity’s constitution, or of the constitutional Law of the entity, the entity has no power to amend the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement.
@ref Article 17(7) (src:455)
@export Whether the deregistered entity has power to amend the preserved charitable purposes or the preserved public benefit statement: none while paragraphs (6) to (11) apply, despite its constitution or constitutional Law
GIVEN `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether some property acquired before the deregistration still remains (Article 17(5))
      `the constitution or the constitutional Law gives the entity a power to amend them` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the entity's constitution or constitutional Law gives it a power to amend the purposes or the statement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(7) the entity has power to amend the preserved purposes or statement` `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` `the constitution or the constitutional Law gives the entity a power to amend them` MEANS
    IF   `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply`
    THEN FALSE
    ELSE `the constitution or the constitutional Law gives the entity a power to amend them`

§§§ `Article 17(8) -- the entity may apply to the court to amend them`

-- src:456 | (8) The entity may apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement, and the court may make such amending order, and any ancillary order, as it considers expedient to ensure that the remaining property continues to be applied for purposes that are, in the opinion of the court, charitable purposes and in a manner that, in the opinion of the court, provides public benefit.
@ref Article 17(8) (src:456)
@export Whether the deregistered entity may apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or the preserved public benefit statement; nothing is permitted once no property remains
GIVEN `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether some property acquired before the deregistration still remains (Article 17(5))
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party after deregistration` `Act of the deregistered entity`
`Art 17(8) the entity may apply to the court to amend the preserved purposes or statement` `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` MEANS
    IF   `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply`
    THEN PARTY `the entity after its deregistration`
         MAY   `apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or the preserved public benefit statement` `the entity after its deregistration`
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- The court may make such amending order, and any ancillary order, as it considers expedient to ensure that the remaining property continues to be applied for purposes that are, in the opinion of the court,
-- charitable purposes and in a manner that, in the opinion of the court, provides public benefit. The court's consideration and its opinions are one determination, supplied as a fact; the content of the order is an input.
@ref Article 17(8) (src:456)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(8) the court may make the amending order` MEANS
    PARTY `the court`
    MAY   `make an amending order` `the court`
    HENCE FULFILLED

@ref Article 17(8) (src:456)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(8) the court may make an ancillary order` MEANS
    PARTY `the court`
    MAY   `make an order ancillary to an amending order` `the court`
    HENCE FULFILLED

-- src:456 | (8) The entity may apply to the court for an order amending the preserved charitable purposes or preserved public benefit statement, and the court may make such amending order, and any ancillary order, as it considers expedient to ensure that the remaining property continues to be applied for purposes that are, in the opinion of the court, charitable purposes and in a manner that, in the opinion of the court, provides public benefit.
@ref Article 17(8) (src:456)
@export Whether and how the court may, on the entity's application, make an amending order and any ancillary order it considers expedient to ensure that the remaining property continues to be applied for purposes that are, in its opinion, charitable and in a manner that, in its opinion, provides public benefit
GIVEN `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether some property acquired before the deregistration still remains (Article 17(5))
      `the entity has applied to the court` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the entity has applied under Article 17(8)
      `the court considers the order expedient to ensure the remaining property is applied for charitable purposes and public benefit` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc The court's consideration that the order is expedient to ensure that the remaining property continues to be applied for purposes that are, in its opinion, charitable purposes and in a manner that, in its opinion, provides public benefit
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(8) the court may make an amending order and any ancillary order` `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` `the entity has applied to the court` `the court considers the order expedient to ensure the remaining property is applied for charitable purposes and public benefit` MEANS
    IF       `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply`
         AND `the entity has applied to the court`
         AND `the court considers the order expedient to ensure the remaining property is applied for charitable purposes and public benefit`
    THEN     `Art 17(8) the court may make the amending order`
         RAND `Art 17(8) the court may make an ancillary order`
    ELSE FULFILLED

§§§ `Article 17(9) to (11) -- the court's powers over the deregistered entity`

-- (9) The court may exercise any of the powers set out in Article 14 in relation to the deregistered entity, as if it were still registered, if, on an application by the Attorney General or a governor of the entity,
-- it appears to the court (a) that there is or has been conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered;
-- or (b) that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of it for the preserved purposes.
-- src:457 | (9) The court may exercise any of the powers set out in Article 14 in relation to the deregistered entity, as if it were still registered, if, on an application by the Attorney General or a governor of the entity, it appears to the court –
-- src:458 | (a) that there is or has been any conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property, that would have been misconduct if the entity had not been deregistered; or
-- src:459 | (b) that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing a proper application of the remaining property for the preserved charitable purposes of the entity and in accordance with the preserved public benefit statement.
@ref Article 17(9) (src:457) (src:458-459)
@export Whether and how the court may exercise a power of Article 14 (one of the orders of Article 14(3)) in relation to the deregistered entity as if it were still registered, on an application by the Attorney General or a governor, where there is or has been conduct that would have been misconduct, or it is necessary or desirable to protect or apply the remaining property
GIVEN `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether some property acquired before the deregistration still remains (Article 17(5))
      applicant IS A `Legal actor`
        @desc Who applied: the Attorney General or a governor of the entity
      `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Article 17(9)(a): whether the conduct in the administration of the entity in relation to the remaining property would have been misconduct within Article 2(10) if the entity had not been deregistered (IP12, applied as if registered)
      `it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the remaining property` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Article 17(9)(b): whether it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power for the purpose of protecting the remaining property or securing its proper application for the preserved purposes and in accordance with the preserved statement
      order IS AN `Order of the court over a charity`
        @desc The order of Article 14(3) in question (IP31)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(9) the court may exercise its Article 14 powers over the deregistered entity` `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply` applicant `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)` `it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the remaining property` order MEANS
    IF       `paragraphs (6) to (11) apply`
         AND (   applicant EQUALS `the Attorney General`
              OR applicant EQUALS `a governor`)
         AND (   `the conduct is misconduct within Article 2(10)`
              OR `it appears to the court that it is necessary or desirable to exercise the power to protect the remaining property`)
    THEN PARTY `the court`
         MAY   `exercise a power of Article 14 over the deregistered entity` `the court` order
         HENCE FULFILLED
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- (10) A governor may not apply under paragraph (9) unless the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor's intention to do so and of the reasons for that intention.
-- "First" is a sequence, not a period: the notice precedes the application, and the fact is that it has been given.
-- src:460 | (10) A governor may not apply under paragraph (9) unless the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor’s intention to do so and of the reasons for that intention.
@ref Article 17(10) (src:460)
@export Whether a governor may apply under Article 17(9): the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it
GIVEN `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the governor gave the Attorney General notice, before applying, of the intention to apply and of the reasons for that intention
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 17(10) the governor may apply under paragraph (9)` `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it` MEANS
    `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it`

-- src:460 | (10) A governor may not apply under paragraph (9) unless the governor has first given notice to the Attorney General of the governor’s intention to do so and of the reasons for that intention.
@ref Article 17(10) (src:460)
@export Whether a governor must not apply under Article 17(9) without first having given the Attorney General notice; applying without it is the failure
GIVEN `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether the governor gave the Attorney General notice, before applying, of the intention to apply and of the reasons for that intention
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(10) a governor may not apply without first notifying the Attorney General` `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it` MEANS
    IF   `the governor has first given the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons for it`
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE PARTY `a governor`
         SHANT `apply to the court under paragraph (9)` `a governor`
         HENCE FULFILLED
         LEST  BREACH BY `a governor` BECAUSE "Article 17(10): a governor applied under paragraph (9) without first giving the Attorney General notice of the intention to apply and of the reasons"

-- (11) The Attorney General may at any time, by giving notice to the governor and the court, take over an application made by a governor under paragraph (9), and may then withdraw or proceed with it as the Attorney General sees fit.
-- No period is stated ("at any time"). After taking over, the two alternatives are permissions of the Attorney General, either of which may be used (the Attorney General "sees fit").
@ref Article 17(11) (src:461)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(11) the Attorney General may then withdraw or proceed with the application` MEANS
        (PARTY `the Attorney General` MAY `withdraw the application` `the Attorney General` HENCE FULFILLED)
    ROR (PARTY `the Attorney General` MAY `proceed with the application` `the Attorney General` HENCE FULFILLED)

-- src:461 | (11) The Attorney General may at any time, by giving notice to the governor and the court, take over an application made by a governor under paragraph (9), and may then withdraw or proceed with the application as the Attorney General sees fit.
@ref Article 17(11) (src:461)
@export Whether and how the Attorney General may at any time take over an application made by a governor under Article 17(9), and then withdraw it or proceed with it as the Attorney General sees fit
GIVEN `a governor has applied under paragraph (9)` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc Whether a governor has made an application under Article 17(9)
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Article 17`
`Art 17(11) the Attorney General may take over the application of a governor` `a governor has applied under paragraph (9)` MEANS
    IF   `a governor has applied under paragraph (9)`
    THEN PARTY `the Attorney General`
         MAY   `take over the application of a governor` `the Attorney General`
         HENCE `Art 17(11) the Attorney General may then withdraw or proceed with the application`
    ELSE FULFILLED
